  "population_diagnosis": "Across r00-r04 the 100-candidate population is effectively frozen: means 4.1295/4.1547/4.1405/4.1962/4.1476 against the incumbent snapshot 4.19627, medians 3.48-3.54, q90 ~6.66-6.78, top10_mean 7.242-7.248, max pinned at ~7.7080 in every round, 100/100 valid and 0 failed attempts (tool:8680d811...). The paired latest-vs-incumbent comparison is degenerate rather than informative: all 100 common-valid, delta mean +9.4e-5 (incumbent minus latest) with q90 1.9e-4 and win rate 0.42 (tool:7710cd0c...), and the frozen-quartile subgroup split shows the same ~1e-4 scale in Q1-Q4 with no quartile gaining (tool:58aa6fb6...). This rules out a strong subgroup-specific compromise and rules out the clipping/matching explanation: clip_factor is 1.0 and clipped_fraction is 0.0 in every interval except a single 0.0008 in the 26-50 window (tool:aea9c3ef..., tool:2f3e73db...). The remaining explanation is the first one: guidance displacement is orders of magnitude too small to correct the mismatch. Mean inside_rms_A is only 0.00092 A (10-25), 0.00047 A (26-50), 0.00019 A (51-75) and 0.00028 A (76-90), i.e. total steering well under 0.002 A, while late-state geometry at step 25 shows core-atom RMS 0.662 A mean (median 0.645, q90 1.074, max 1.563 A) versus the fixed protein frame (tool:323ac8af...). Mismatch therefore persists essentially uncorrected into the late checkpoints, and the cumulative loss grows monotonically (0.173 -> 0.200 -> 0.234 -> 0.279 -> 0.316) while gradient_drift_cosine turns increasingly negative (mean -0.020 early, -0.082 mid, -0.148 and -0.134 late), consistent with guidance that is too weak early to be productive and later mildly discordant with the native gradient. Search budget for this planning round: 4 hypotheses x 100 unselected candidates = 400 new attempts, all on the same pre-round incumbent snapshot; this is separate from and adds to the 5 already-logged rounds (r00-r04, 500 attempts) and must not be conflated with per-chain TO